Z||000000||2M4 Dale||10-07-1999||01:25 PM||||I have a 87 coupe, 4cly, 5spd. The slave cylinder just gave up the ghost. Should I replace the master cylinder and lines as well as the slave? The slave cylinder had a lot of rust and muddy lookin' brake fluid in it. I have not looked at the inside of the master cylinder yet but I imagine it's just as bad as the slave. Can I blow the lines with air or use some kind of solvent to clean them. Also would I see any difference between DOT3 or DOT4 brake fluid? Any suggestions would be helpful. ||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||10-07-1999||01:50 PM||||wrong posting area guy.<P>head over to the tech forum, and use search. there are several threads to chose from. one or more should answer your questions.<P>yes, you can blow the lines out, but make sure there's something there to catch it. Brake fluid eats paint. You shouldn't need solvents to clean the lines, just flush a bit of new fluid thru.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-07-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||10-07-1999||05:30 PM||||Short answer - I'd try replacing just the slave, bleed the system, and see how it drives.  If it's still screwy, you can always replace the master then and you're only out an hour's work and a jug of brake fluid.<P>I had to replace the slave on my 88 Coupe 30K miles ago, but am still using the original master cylinder with fine results.  Why spend money you don't have to?  See you on the tech ??? page!||||reg||1
Z||000003||2M4 Dale||10-08-1999||12:01 AM||||Wow! maybe I should go to the tech forum. Sorry guys I'm pretty new to your Fiero forum. But don't worry I get the hang of it.   Thanks for the answers to my Question anyway. I think I blow the lines, put on my new slave cylinder and see if I can go for a drive this weekend.||||reg||3
